# Profile stem AND saved-world directory: the server reads
# Zomboid/Server/${SERVER_NAME}.ini and saves to Saves/Multiplayer/${SERVER_NAME}.
# WARNING: changing this on a server that already has a world makes PZ start a
# NEW EMPTY world. The old one is not deleted, but it will not be loaded either.
# On first boot start.sh derives ${SERVER_NAME}.* from the Server/server.* template.
SERVER_NAME=server

# --- Passwords ----------------------------------------------------------
# Four separate secrets, four different jobs. Only SERVER_PASSWORD is meant to
# be shared with the people you play with:
#   CODE_SERVER_PASSWORD → web IDE login on :8443            (keep to yourself)
#   ADMIN_PASSWORD       → the in-game `admin` account       (keep to yourself)
#   SERVER_PASSWORD      → what players type to join         (share with friends)
#   RCON_PASSWORD        → remote console                    (keep to yourself)
# Generate each with: openssl rand -base64 24

# In-game admin account, passed to the server as -adminpassword. Nothing else
# uses it.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me

# Login for code-server on :8443. Deliberately NOT the same value as
# ADMIN_PASSWORD: the port is published over plain HTTP with no TLS in front of
# it, so this secret is the most exposed one here. Required even when
# ENABLE_CODE_SERVER=false, so enabling the IDE can never leave it open.
CODE_SERVER_PASSWORD=change-me

# --- Memory -------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO ceilings, and you need both. They are not the same thing:
#   PZ_HEAP       → -Xmx. Only the JVM heap: world, chunks, zombies.
#   PZ_MEM_LIMIT  → compose mem_limit. The WHOLE container: the heap PLUS JVM
#                   non-heap (metaspace, code cache, ~1 thread per player,
#                   direct buffers), PZ's native/tcmalloc allocations and
#                   code-server. That extra is ~1.5 GB.
#
# THREE conditions, not one. Breaking the second is what froze the 6 GB VM:
#
#   1. PZ_MEM_LIMIT ~= PZ_HEAP + 1.5 GB      (the container's own overhead)
#   2. PZ_MEM_LIMIT <= VM RAM - 2 GB         (what the HOST needs to stay alive)
#   3. PZ_CPUS      <= nproc - 1             (so sshd always has a core)
#
# Rule 2 used to read "at most the VM's RAM", and that is not a safety margin at
# all. 4608m of 6144m left the host ~1.5 GB for kernel + systemd + dockerd +
# sshd + page cache. PZ mmaps ~2 GB of assets and map chunks straight through
# that budget, and with no swap in the cgroup AND none on the VM the kernel had
# nowhere to evict anonymous pages: instead of a clean OOM kill it livelocked
# reclaiming, and the entire VM hung — SSH included — every time PZ started.
#
# Note the host reserve in rule 2 (2 GB) is LARGER than the container overhead
# in rule 1 (1.5 GB), and that difference is load-bearing: at 1.5 GB on both
# sides, 4608m on a 6 GB VM lands exactly on the boundary and passes — i.e. the
# rule would still bless the configuration that froze the machine. The page
# cache PZ streams its map through is charged to the host side of the ledger.
#
# Rule 3 is the other half of the same freeze: with cpus == nproc, the JVM's GC
# and JIT pools plus chunk streaming can occupy every vCPU at boot and sshd
# never gets scheduled. A busy VM and a hung VM look identical from outside.
#
#   VM RAM | PZ_HEAP | PZ_MEM_LIMIT | PZ_MEMSWAP_LIMIT | PZ_CPUS
#   -------+---------+--------------+------------------+--------
#    6 GB  |   2g    |    3584m     |      4608m       |   3      <- games-server
#    8 GB  |   3g    |    4608m     |      5632m       |   3
#   12 GB  |   5g    |    6656m     |      7680m       |   6
#   16 GB  |   8g    |    9728m     |     10752m       |   6
#
# start.sh prints "Memory budget: heap ... | container limit ... | host RAM ...
# | host swap ..." and "CPU budget: ..." at boot, and warns on each of the three
# rules above plus a host with no swap — check the logs after any change here.
PZ_HEAP=2g
PZ_MEM_LIMIT=3584m
# Total memory + swap for the container: ~1 GB above PZ_MEM_LIMIT on purpose.
# This is a REVERSAL of the old "keep it equal so we fail fast" setting — see
# the rule-2 note above for why failing fast is not what actually happened.
# A container that spills a gigabyte to disk is slow; a VM you cannot SSH into
# is down. Needs host swap to mean anything: run vm-hardening.sh once.
PZ_MEMSWAP_LIMIT=4608m
# vCPUs the container may use. Keep it at nproc - 1 (rule 3 above); never above
# the VM's nproc. start.sh also passes this to the JVM as
# -XX:ActiveProcessorCount, because the JVM otherwise sizes its thread pools
# against the host's CPU count and ignores this quota entirely.
PZ_CPUS=3
# -Xms, the heap the JVM commits at startup. Deliberately much smaller than
# PZ_HEAP: setting them equal made the JVM claim the full heap in the first
# second of boot, concurrently with everything else starting, which is precisely
# when the VM died. Leave this alone unless you know why you are changing it.
PZ_HEAP_MIN=512m
#
# REQUIRED ON THE VM: none of the limits above give you a safety net on a host
# with no swap and no OOM handler. Run `sudo bash vm-hardening.sh` on the VM once
# (swapfile, vm.min_free_kbytes, earlyoom, sshd OOMScoreAdjust, persistent
# journald). Without it, an overshoot still takes the whole machine down and
# leaves no evidence behind.
#
# NOTE: .env is gitignored and does NOT travel in upload_to_server.sh's tar, so
# these values have to be set by hand in the VM's own .env. A VM whose .env
# predates these keys silently falls back to the compose defaults.
#
# Changing the values above only needs `docker compose up -d`. Changing start.sh
# needs `docker compose up -d --build`: the script is COPYed into the image, so
# the container keeps running the old one until the image is rebuilt. The
# "[start.sh] revision: ..." line at the top of the logs says which copy is live.

# --- Web IDE ------------------------------------------------------------
# code-server on :8443 runs inside the game container, sharing its cgroup and
# its memory limit, and costs ~300-500 MB. Off by default so that budget goes to
# the world. Flip to true, `docker compose up -d`, edit the .ini, flip it back.
ENABLE_CODE_SERVER=false

# RCON password. The port is not published (access via `docker exec`), but this
# is also what start.sh uses to send an ordered `quit` on `docker stop` — leave
# it empty and shutdown degrades to a plain SIGTERM.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 24
# Usage: docker exec -it project-zomboid-server pz-rcon players
RCON_PASSWORD=change-me
RCON_PORT=27015

# --- Server identity ----------------------------------------------------
# Written into the .ini at boot. Leave empty to keep whatever is already in the
# volume — which is how an existing deployment stays untouched.
#
# SERVER_PASSWORD is the join password: it lands in the .ini as `Password=` and
# is what players are prompted for. Leave it empty and the server is open to
# anyone who knows the IP and port.
# CAVEAT: because "empty means don't touch", you cannot clear it again by
# blanking this line — once written into the volume it stays. To remove it,
# edit `Password=` in Zomboid/Server/${SERVER_NAME}.ini through code-server.
SERVER_PASSWORD=change-me
PUBLIC_NAME=
PUBLIC_DESCRIPTION=

# --- Version ------------------------------------------------------------
# The Steam branch, applied via `app_update -beta <branch>`. Named branches are
# frozen builds; the public branch is not.
#   (empty)  → public branch — currently Build 42.20.2 (buildid 24574884)
#   42.19    → Build 42.19.1  (buildid 24438606)
#   legacy41 → Build 41.78.20 (buildid 24432954)
# List the current branches with:
#   curl -s https://api.steamcmd.net/v1/info/380870 | jq '.data["380870"].depots.branches'
#
# We track `public` because TIS never published a frozen 42.20 branch, and the
# Steam client is on 42.20.2 — the client refuses to join a server on a
# different build. There is NO way to pin a build outside a named branch:
# download_depot fails under `login anonymous` with "Depot download failed :
# missing license for depot (No subscription)" — an anonymous account is
# licensed for the app but not for its depots. Do not reintroduce
# PZ_MANIFEST_*; it cannot work without credentials for an account that owns
# the game.
#
# .env is gitignored and does NOT travel in upload_to_server.sh's tar, so
# changing this on the VM means editing its .env by hand.
PZ_BRANCH=

# Because `public` moves on its own, the pin is on disk instead of on the
# branch: start.sh records the installed branch in
# /opt/pzserver/.pz-installed-branch and, with PZ_AUTO_UPDATE=false, skips
# SteamCMD entirely on later boots. So a restart can never bump the server past
# friends who have not updated their client yet.
#   false → install once, then stay on that build (recommended)
#   true  → run app_update on every boot, always the branch's latest
# To take an update: set true, `docker compose up -d`, confirm the new build in
# the logs, set false again. Changing PZ_BRANCH forces a reinstall regardless.
PZ_AUTO_UPDATE=false
